const char ART_TEXT[] =
    "Tightrope Dancers\nHans Christian\nAndersen (1805-1875)\nundated, paper cut\nOdense\x00"""
    "Hans Christian\nAndersen, who wrote\nThe Little Mermaid\nand The Ugly\nDuckling, also made\nart with scissors.\nWhen he told a story\nto a room of\nchildren, he cut a\nfolded sheet of\npaper as he talked,\nand at the end of\nthe tale he unfolded\nit and gave it away.\nHe made at least a\nthousand paper cuts.\nAbout 400 survive,\nmost of them in\nOdense, the town\nwhere he was born.\x00"""
    "Of a Flea, from\nMicrographia\nRobert Hooke\n(1635-1703)\n1665, engraving\x00"""
    "In 1665 Robert Hooke\nshowed the world\nwhat he saw through\nhis microscope.\nMicrographia was the\nfirst major book of\nthe Royal Society\nand the first\nscientific\nbest-seller. Its\nplates of insects\nfold out larger than\nthe big book itself,\nand it gave biology\nthe word cell.\nSamuel Pepys called\nit \"the most\ningenious book that\never I read in my\nlife\".\x00"""
    "The Swan, No. 1\nHilma af Klint\n(1862-1944)\n1915, oil on canvas\x00"""
    "Hilma af Klint was a\nrespected Stockholm\npainter of\nlandscapes and\nportraits. In\nprivate she painted\nsomething else. With\nfour friends, The\nFive, she held\nseances, and she\nsaid spirits she\ncalled the High\nMasters asked her\nfor paintings for a\nTemple. From 1906 to\n1915 she made 196 of\nthem, many abstract,\nbefore Kandinsky.\nSure the world was\nnot ready, she\ndirected that they\nstay unseen until\ntwenty years after\nher death. Her\npictures often pair\nopposites: here a\nwhite swan and a\nblack one meet.\x00"""
    "Dictyota dichotoma\nAnna Atkins\n(1799-1871)\n1843, cyanotype\x00"""
    "In 1842 the\nastronomer John\nHerschel, a friend\nof the family,\ninvented the\ncyanotype: paper\nthat turns deep blue\nin the sun. Within a\nyear Anna Atkins was\nlaying dried seaweed\nstraight onto it,\nand where the plant\nlay, the paper\nstayed white. In\nOctober 1843 she\npublished the first\npart of Photographs\nof British Algae,\noften called the\nfirst book\nillustrated with\nphotographs, with\nhandwritten text.\nOnly 17 copies are\nknown.\x00"""
    "Takiyasha the Witch\nand the Skeleton\nSpectre\nUtagawa Kuniyoshi\n(1798-1861)\nc. 1844, woodblock\x00"""
    "Princess Takiyasha,\ndaughter of the\nrebel lord Taira no\nMasakado, reads a\nspell from a scroll.\nA skeleton rears out\nof the dark and\ncrashes through the\npalace blinds to\nfrighten Mitsukuni,\nthe emperor's man.\nIn the old tale she\nsummons many\nlife-sized\nskeletons. Kuniyoshi\nmade it one giant\nacross three sheets.\nA century later the\ngiant skeleton ghost\nof Japanese comics,\nthe gashadokuro, was\ndrawn from this\nprint.\x00"""
    "Passiflora\nlaurifolia\nMary Delany\n(1700-1788)\n1777, paper collage\x00"""
    "Mary Delany was 71,\nand a widow for the\nsecond time, when\nshe began cutting\nflowers out of\ncoloured paper.\nPetal by petal she\nglued them onto\nblack, botanically\naccurate, and\nfriends sent her\nflowers to cut. She\ncalled them her\npaper mosaicks and\nmade 985 of them,\nuntil her eyesight\nfailed at 88. They\nare now in the\nBritish Museum.\x00"""
    "The Eye, Like a\nStrange Balloon,\nMounts Toward\nInfinity\nOdilon Redon\n(1840-1916)\n1882, lithograph\x00"""
    "For years Odilon\nRedon worked almost\nonly in black:\ncharcoal drawings\nand lithographs he\ncalled his noirs,\nfull of eyes,\nfloating heads and\nspiders with human\nfaces. Few noticed\nuntil 1884, when\nHuysmans' cult novel\nAgainst Nature gave\nits hero a\ncollection of them.\nThis is the first\nplate of an album\ndedicated to Edgar\nAllan Poe. In the\n1890s Redon turned\nto colour, and after\n1900 he made no more\nnoirs.\x00"""
    "Spiders, ants and a\nhummingbird\nMaria Sibylla Merian\n(1647-1717)\n1705, engraving,\ncoloured by hand\x00"""
    "In 1699, at 52,\nMaria Sibylla Merian\nsold 255 of her\npaintings and sailed\nfrom Amsterdam to\nSuriname with her\ndaughter Dorothea,\nto study insects\nwhere they live. Her\nbook of 1705 showed\nthem at every stage\nof life, when many\nstill believed\ninsects were born\nfrom mud. One plate\nshows a huge spider\neating a\nhummingbird. It is\nwhy such tarantulas\nare still called\nbird-eating spiders,\nthough they rarely\neat birds.\x00"""
    "Laziness (La\nParesse)\nF\x06""lix Vallotton\n(1865-1925)\n1896, woodcut\x00"""
    "F\x06""lix Vallotton, a\nSwiss painter in\nParis, saw the\nJapanese prints at\nthe 1889 World's\nFair, Hokusai's\nabove all, and they\nchanged his work.\nHis woodcuts are\nbroad masses of\nblack and white with\nalmost no detail: he\nmade a precise\ndrawing, then\nsimplified, and\nsimplified again. A\ncritic called it the\nrenaissance of the\nwoodcut. Here a\nwoman stretches\nacross a patterned\nbed toward a cat.\x00"""
    "Night Scene in the\nYoshiwara\nKatsushika Oi\n(c. 1800-c. 1866)\nc. 1818-1844,\ncolour on paper\x00"""
    "Oi was the daughter\nof Hokusai. She\nmarried a painter,\nlaughed at his work,\nthe story goes, and\nleft him after three\nyears to paint\nbeside her father in\ntheir famously\nuntidy home. She was\nknown for her\npictures of\nbeautiful women.\nHere, in the dark,\nvisitors gather at\nthe lattice of the\npleasure quarter,\nwhere courtesans sit\nin the light. She\nhid her name in the\nlanterns: three of\nthem carry O, i and\nEi.\x00"""
    "Lily, from Lily and\nStock\nPhilipp Otto Runge\n(1777-1810)\n1804-1806, paper cut\x00"""
    "Philipp Otto Runge\nwas a sickly child\nwho often missed\nschool, and his\nmother taught him to\ncut silhouettes. He\ncut flowers and\nanimals from white\npaper all his life.\nHe became, with\nCaspar David\nFriedrich, a leading\npainter of German\nRomanticism,\nimagined all colours\nas a sphere, and\nwrote down two folk\ntales the Brothers\nGrimm later printed,\namong them The\nFisherman and His\nWife. He died of\ntuberculosis at 33.\x00"""
    "Vertigo\nL\x06""on Spilliaert\n(1881-1946)\n1908, on paper\nMu.ZEE, Ostend\x00"""
    "L\x06""on Spilliaert,\nborn in the Belgian\nseaside town of\nOstend, was mostly\nself-taught, sickly\nand shy. Stomach\nulcers kept him\nawake, so he walked\nthe empty promenade,\ndykes and beaches at\nnight, and drew what\nhe found: lone\nfigures, endless\nwalls, the sea in\nthe dark. In Vertigo\na woman stands on a\nstaircase that seems\nto spin away beneath\nher.\x00"""
    "The Hanging, from\nThe Miseries of War\nJacques Callot\n(1592-1635)\n1633, etching\x00"""
    "In 1633 Cardinal\nRichelieu's army\noccupied Lorraine,\nCallot's homeland.\nThat year he\npublished 18 small\netchings, each about\n8 by 18 cm, of what\nsoldiers did to\nordinary people in\nthe Thirty Years'\nWar: looting,\nburning, torture,\nand then the\nsoldiers' own\npunishment, as here.\nThey have been\ncalled the first\nanti-war statement\nin European art.\nGoya probably owned\na set; his Disasters\nof War followed two\ncenturies later.\x00"""
    "Millions of Cats\nWanda G\x07""g\n(1893-1946)\n1928, picture book\x00"""
    "A very old man goes\nout to find one cat\nfor his wife and\ncomes home with\n\"hundreds of cats,\nthousands of cats,\nmillions and\nbillions and\ntrillions of cats\".\nWanda G\x07""g first told\nthe tale to friends'\nchildren. Published\nin 1928, with\nlettering by her\nbrother Howard, it\nwon a Newbery Honor\nand is the oldest\nAmerican picture\nbook still in print.\nShe pioneered the\ndouble-page spread,\nletting pictures\nsweep across both\npages, like these\nhills.\x00""";
